---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';

const config = `{
  "label": "Line items total",
  "apiName": "line_items_total",
  "type": "rollup_summary",
  "rollupObjectApiName": "deal_line_item",
  "rollupRelationField": "deal",
  "rollupType": "SUM",
  "rollupField": "subtotal",
  "rollupFilter": "NOT ISBLANK(unit_price)"
}`;

const TYPES = [
  ['COUNT', 'Number of matching child records', 'Ignores rollupField'],
  ['SUM', 'Total of a numeric child field', 'Requires rollupField'],
  ['AVG', 'Mean of a numeric child field', 'Requires rollupField'],
  ['MIN', 'Smallest value', 'Requires rollupField'],
  ['MAX', 'Largest value', 'Requires rollupField'],
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="Rollup Summaries"
  description="Aggregating child records onto a parent: configuration, filters and constraints."
>
  <h1>Rollup Summaries</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A rollup summary aggregates child records onto their parent — a deal's total
    line-item value, a client's open deal count. The value is computed and stored
    on the parent whenever a child changes, so list views and filters read it
    directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <pre><code>{config}</code></pre>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>rollupObjectApiName</code></td><td>The child object to aggregate.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rollupRelationField</code></td><td>The lookup on the child pointing back at this parent.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rollupType</code></td><td>The aggregate: <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rollupField</code></td><td>The child field to aggregate. Ignored by <code>COUNT</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rollupFilter</code></td><td>Optional expression — only children where it is <strong>true</strong> are counted.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Aggregate types</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Computes</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {TYPES.map(([t, computes, notes]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{t}</code></td><td>{computes}</td><td>{notes}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The relationship requirement</h2>
  <p>
    A rollup only makes sense over records that <em>belong</em> to the parent. Here
    that means the child's relationship field must be a lookup with
    <strong>cascade delete</strong> enabled — the child is removed with its parent,
    so an aggregate can never include orphaned rows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only objects with such a relationship are offered as the summarised object. If
    the child object you want isn't listed, the relationship field on it needs
    cascade delete turned on.
  </p>

  <div class="warning">
    <strong>Only numeric child fields can be aggregated</strong> by
    <code>SUM</code> and <code>AVG</code> — <code>number</code>,
    <code>currency</code>, <code>percent</code> or a numeric <code>formula</code>.
    <code>COUNT</code> aggregates nothing and so has no such requirement.
  </div>

  <h2>Filters</h2>
  <p>
    <code>rollupFilter</code> uses the same expression syntax as
    <a href="/concepts/formula-fields">formula fields</a>, evaluated against each
    child record. Unlike a validation rule, the direction here is
    <strong>inclusive</strong>: a child counts when the filter is true.
  </p>
  <pre><code>stage == "won"
NOT ISBLANK(unit_price)
quantity &gt; 0 AND status != "cancelled"</code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>An unevaluable filter excludes the child.</strong> If the expression
    throws for a given record, that record is left out and a warning is logged —
    chosen so a broken filter under-reports rather than silently inflating a total
    someone is invoicing against.
  </div>

  <h2>Evaluation order</h2>
  <p>
    Rollups are computed <strong>before</strong> formula fields on save, so a
    formula may read a rollup value. That is how a currency-formatted total is
    built: a hidden rollup does the aggregation, and a formula field presents it.
  </p>
  <pre><code>line_items_selling_total   rollup_summary, SUM, isHidden: true
order_value                formula, ROUND(line_items_selling_total, 2), currency</code></pre>
  <p>
    The rollup carries the number; the formula gives it a return type and therefore
    a display format. Marking the rollup <code>isHidden</code> keeps it out of
    layouts and column pickers while remaining referenceable.
  </p>

  <h2>When values refresh</h2>
  <p>
    A parent's rollups are recomputed when a child is created, updated or deleted,
    within the same transaction as the child's write — so the parent is never
    briefly wrong. They are also recomputed when the parent itself is saved.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong><code>COUNT</code> with no filter never loads the children.</strong> It
    issues a count query instead. Any other aggregate, or any filter, has to read
    the child documents to evaluate it — worth knowing when a parent has very many
    children.
  </div>

  <h2>Constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rollup values are read-only over the API.</li>
    <li>Rollup fields are excluded from CSV export.</li>
    <li>A rollup cannot aggregate across tenants — the child query is always tenant-scoped.</li>
    <li>Soft-deleted children are excluded.</li>
    <li>
      Rollups are treated as numeric for list-view filtering, which is correct for
      <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code> and <code>AVG</code>. A
      <code>MIN</code>/<code>MAX</code> over a date field is a date, so filter those
      with care.
    </li>
  </ul>
</MainLayout>
